  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter the relatively cloudy start to the day, there has been \nenough clearing and instability to allow showers and \nthunderstorms to develop this afternoon well ahead of an \napproaching cold front. Current mesoanalysis shows 1000-1500 \nJ/kg of MLCAPE, 2000-2500 J/kg of SBCAPE, steep low-level lapse \nrates, 0-6km bulk shear around 30 knots and some pockets of \n0-3km SRH of 100 m2/s2 across the CWA. As a result, some locally\nstrong to severe storms have developed with damaging winds and \nlarge hail the main concerns. Given a little bit of spin in the \nlow-levels, an isolated, weak tornado cannot be ruled with any \nstorm. \n\nThe severe storm threat is expected to shift from the Finger \nLakes Region into the Southern Tier, NE PA and the southern \nCatskills as the rest of the afternoon/evening progresses. There\nis the potential for localized heavy rain with PWATs around \n1.40-1.50 inches and some potential training of showers and \nstorms as well. This does lead to an isolated flash flood risk, \nwith some storms lining up currently along the southern/eastern  \nFinger Lakes, and later on this evening in the Catskills and \nportions of NE PA. \n\nWhile the severe threat looks to wane by 9-10PM this evening, \nshowers an an additional isolated storm are expected to linger \ninto the overnight before diminishing. The actual cold front \nitself is expected to move through tomorrow morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the cold front continues to push east tomorrow and high \npressure starts to builds in at the surface, the upper level \ntrough will still be lagging overhead. This can lead to an \nisolated shower or two, mainly through midday and across the \nhigher terrain of CNY before noticeably lower PWATs settle in \nand dry conditions are expected the rest of the day and through\nthe upcoming weekend. \n\nHigh temperatures tomorrow and Saturday will be mainly in the \nmid and upper 70s. Sunday is expected to trend warmer as the \nflow becomes more westerly with highs in the upper 70s and lower\n80s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA frontal boundary approaching from the north on Monday may \nstall out near the area through the first part of next week. \nThis can lead to multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms \nthrough Wednesday. During this same timeframe, the northern \nperiphery of a building ridge is also expected to be near or \nover the CWA allowing for a few waves of energy embedded in the \nflow to move through enhancing the risk for scattered showers \nand thunderstorms. High temperatures early next week look to be \nmainly in the upper 70s and low 80s.",
